00:00:45.953 --> 00:00:47.663
So special, thanks to bank of America,

00:00:47.663 --> 00:00:51.233
Google.org AT&amp;T, Fastly and Novartis.

00:00:51.233 --> 00:00:54.663
So with that, I'm super excited to invite

00:00:54.663 --> 00:00:57.683
or to introduce a Nikole
Hannah-Jones, a journalist

00:00:57.683 --> 00:01:00.333
at New York Times, creator, leader,

00:01:00.333 --> 00:01:02.865
founder of The 1619 Project.

00:01:02.865 --> 00:01:05.041
Nikole, thanks so much for joining.

00:01:05.041 --> 00:01:06.041
- Thank you.

00:01:07.413 --> 00:01:09.120
- So there there's a lot to talk about.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:11.463
And I wanna encourage
anyone who's listening.

00:01:11.463 --> 00:01:13.713
You can go on the YouTube or
the Facebook message boards,

00:01:13.713 --> 00:01:15.693
and we'll try to surface as
many questions as possible

00:01:15.693 --> 00:01:18.723
to Nikole and myself, if it's interesting,

00:01:18.723 --> 00:01:21.533
but Nikole, maybe a
good place to start is,

00:01:21.533 --> 00:01:24.393
what is The 1619 Project?

00:01:24.393 --> 00:01:26.956
And what is the significance of that year?

00:01:27.873 --> 00:01:31.533
- Sure, thank you so much
for having me on today.

00:01:31.533 --> 00:01:36.533
So The 1619 Project published
a year ago in August

00:01:36.943 --> 00:01:40.303
and it is a project that really examines

00:01:40.303 --> 00:01:42.153
the modern legacy of slavery.

00:01:42.153 --> 00:01:45.113
So it looks at all of
these different aspects

00:01:45.113 --> 00:01:48.533
of contemporary American
life and uses history

00:01:48.533 --> 00:01:52.693
to trace these various
aspects of our society

00:01:52.693 --> 00:01:54.570
back to the institution of slavery

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:59.323
and the anti-black racism that
developed to justify slavery.

00:01:59.323 --> 00:02:03.063
So the main premise is that
slavery was foundational

00:02:03.063 --> 00:02:06.103
to the United States
and that almost nothing

00:02:06.103 --> 00:02:09.263
has been left untouched
by that institution

00:02:09.263 --> 00:02:11.603
and our decision to be a country founded

00:02:11.603 --> 00:02:14.553
on both ideas of freedom and slavery.

00:02:14.553 --> 00:02:18.443
So 1619 is the year
that the first Africans

00:02:18.443 --> 00:02:20.693
were sold into colonial America.

00:02:20.693 --> 00:02:24.063
It was in that year that a
ship called the White Lion

00:02:24.063 --> 00:02:29.063
brought a cargo of between
20 and 30 and Golden's

00:02:29.083 --> 00:02:32.113
who had been stolen and
they were sold into slavery

00:02:32.113 --> 00:02:33.473
to the Virginia colonists.

00:02:33.473 --> 00:02:36.513
So that year marks the
beginning of slavery

00:02:36.513 --> 00:02:38.616
in what would become the United States.

00:02:39.973 --> 00:02:42.073
- And it's fascinating.

00:02:42.073 --> 00:02:45.100
I mean, I've been reading a
lot of what's been written

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:47.613
and it's a whole, it's
not just one article

00:02:47.613 --> 00:02:49.663
or one series of writing,
it's even evolving

00:02:49.663 --> 00:02:52.653
into a kind of a supplemental
curriculum of sorts.

00:02:52.653 --> 00:02:56.603
What did you feel or you and
The New York Times Magazine

00:02:56.603 --> 00:02:58.463
feel was the gap?

00:02:58.463 --> 00:03:01.103
What was missing in our public knowledge

00:03:01.103 --> 00:03:03.663
or the education system?

00:03:03.663 --> 00:03:06.793
- Sure, so I guess I should
have said a little bit more

00:03:06.793 --> 00:03:09.223
about what the project consists of.

00:03:09.223 --> 00:03:11.303
The project is an entire issue

00:03:11.303 --> 00:03:12.743
of The New York Times Magazine.

00:03:12.743 --> 00:03:16.563
And that issue has about 10
essays written by writers

00:03:16.563 --> 00:03:19.773
and historians and each one
examines a different aspect

00:03:19.773 --> 00:03:21.153
of American society.

00:03:21.153 --> 00:03:23.733
So I wrote the opening
essay that was on democracy.

00:03:23.733 --> 00:03:27.003
There's an essay on capitalism,on
why we consume so much

00:03:27.003 --> 00:03:29.883
sugar, on why we're the only
Western industrialized country

00:03:29.883 --> 00:03:32.453
without universal healthcare, on traffic.

00:03:32.453 --> 00:03:37.453
So really the key was we
wanted to surprise readers

00:03:38.213 --> 00:03:40.633
who we have been taught when you ask

00:03:40.633 --> 00:03:42.923
kind of what's that, what's been absent?

00:03:42.923 --> 00:03:45.553
The way that we're typically
taught about slavery

00:03:45.553 --> 00:03:48.743
is kinda as an asterix
to the American story.

00:03:48.743 --> 00:03:52.453
That it was an unfortunate
thing that we did,

00:03:52.453 --> 00:03:57.453
but it wasn't that big
of a part of America

00:03:58.645 --> 00:04:03.645
that it's more of a marginal
story that we tell ourselves.

00:04:03.883 --> 00:04:07.043
And because of that, we've
never really confronted

00:04:07.043 --> 00:04:10.077
what it means to be a nation
that was founded in slavery

00:04:10.077 --> 00:04:12.411
and the ongoing legacy.

00:04:12.411 --> 00:04:15.367
So what we try to do with
the magazine was again,

00:04:15.367 --> 00:04:19.384
to show that whether we
acknowledge that legacy or not,

00:04:19.384 --> 00:04:23.250
it operates often invisibly
all across our society

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:26.264
and it has these effects
all across our society.

00:04:26.264 --> 00:04:31.238
The project also included a
special section of the newspaper

00:04:31.238 --> 00:04:33.467
and that special section
was done in partnership

00:04:33.467 --> 00:04:36.373
with the National Museum
of African American History

00:04:36.373 --> 00:04:38.165
and Culture in Washington, DC.

00:04:38.165 --> 00:04:42.053
And the special section
is really a visual history

00:04:42.053 --> 00:04:44.608
of slavery using
artifacts from the museum.

00:04:44.608 --> 00:04:47.554
And between those two,
they were complimentary

00:04:47.554 --> 00:04:48.970
because the magazine was really

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:51.745
looking at the modern and ongoing legacy.

00:04:51.745 --> 00:04:54.642
And the special section
was giving us the history

00:04:54.642 --> 00:04:58.007
that we don't get because
we are just so poorly taught

00:04:58.007 --> 00:04:59.296
about this history.

00:04:59.296 --> 00:05:01.819
The Southern Poverty Law Center.

00:05:01.819 --> 00:05:04.893
Research has shown that only
8% of American high school

00:05:04.893 --> 00:05:06.851
students even know that the civil war

00:05:06.851 --> 00:05:07.923
was fought over slavery.

00:05:07.923 --> 00:05:11.350
So I think that gives us
a good idea of how poorly

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:12.522
we've been taught this.

00:05:12.522 --> 00:05:16.048
And then the final part of
The 1619 Project is a podcast

00:05:16.048 --> 00:05:18.813
and the podcast is called 1619.

00:05:18.813 --> 00:05:23.312
And it goes over the
course of five episodes,

00:05:23.312 --> 00:05:27.103
several of the areas in
the project that we address

00:05:27.103 --> 00:05:32.103
and the reaction has been
really people are very surprised

00:05:32.259 --> 00:05:33.643
by what they learned in this project.

00:05:33.643 --> 00:05:37.013
They realize how little we've been taught

00:05:37.013 --> 00:05:40.045
about an institution that
has had such an influence

00:05:40.045 --> 00:05:42.556
on the society that we've become.

00:05:43.749 --> 00:05:46.158
- I have to admit, you
know, I considered myself,

00:05:46.158 --> 00:05:48.881
someone who's reasonably well
versed in American history.

00:05:48.881 --> 00:05:50.989
But the more reading I did,

00:05:50.989 --> 00:05:53.394
especially with The 1619
Project, the more I realized

00:05:53.394 --> 00:05:58.394
that yeah, I had a thread
of the narrative but not

00:05:58.492 --> 00:06:00.637
the complete picture by any
stretch of the imagination.

00:06:00.637 --> 00:06:02.238
You know, there's really
interesting things.

00:06:02.238 --> 00:06:05.910
And I think, even word choice is sometimes

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:08.348
folk can get skeptical of
all if you call something,

00:06:08.348 --> 00:06:10.438
this or that, but you know, when you refer

00:06:10.438 --> 00:06:12.832
to plantations, I grew up in Louisiana

00:06:12.832 --> 00:06:14.748
and obviously, we had
plantations, people would visit,

00:06:14.748 --> 00:06:16.235
we would take family
members down, take pictures,

00:06:16.235 --> 00:06:18.120
people have weddings at them.

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:19.570
But you kinda call them for what they are,

00:06:19.570 --> 00:06:20.943
forced labor camps.

00:06:20.943 --> 00:06:25.273
You point out that the great
majority of the framers

00:06:25.273 --> 00:06:29.350
of the constitution own slaves,
many of them lived in homes

00:06:29.350 --> 00:06:32.466
and they were continuing to perpetuate it.

00:06:33.493 --> 00:06:35.393
Do you think, I've always had at least

00:06:35.393 --> 00:06:37.334
maybe a rationalization in my mind,

00:06:37.334 --> 00:06:39.274
obviously my family came as immigrants,

00:06:39.274 --> 00:06:41.747
but I have always been a kind
of an American exceptionalist,

00:06:41.747 --> 00:06:43.942
like look at the enlightenment
and the declaration

00:06:43.942 --> 00:06:45.611
of independence, how beautiful this is.

00:06:45.611 --> 00:06:48.171
There was clearly this like,
amazing group of people

00:06:48.171 --> 00:06:51.123
who came together and they were
imperfect, but they created

00:06:51.123 --> 00:06:53.448
something that could become
a more perfect union.

00:06:53.448 --> 00:06:56.900
Do you think that the
framers of the constitution

00:06:56.900 --> 00:07:00.102
or the founders of our
country, they appreciated some

00:07:00.102 --> 00:07:04.090
of the hypocrisy or ironies
or tensions and that maybe

00:07:04.090 --> 00:07:05.893
they were uncomfortable with them

00:07:05.893 --> 00:07:08.401
or do you think it was
just they compartmentalize

00:07:08.401 --> 00:07:11.042
their mind in this in a certain way?

00:07:11.042 --> 00:07:13.204
- I think it was both.

00:07:13.204 --> 00:07:18.204
Certainly the framers understood
the hypocrisy of arguing

00:07:18.380 --> 00:07:21.603
that they deserve to be
liberated from great Britain

00:07:21.603 --> 00:07:25.659
while holding 1/5 of a population
in absolute bottom bondage

00:07:25.659 --> 00:07:26.492
and chattel slavery.

00:07:26.492 --> 00:07:28.843
You know, one of the things we hear a lot

00:07:28.843 --> 00:07:32.727
is we shouldn't judge people back then

00:07:32.727 --> 00:07:37.203
by today's morals and standards,
but we should be clear.

00:07:37.203 --> 00:07:41.063
There was never a time
when we did not know

00:07:41.063 --> 00:07:42.509
that it was wrong to
own other human beings,

00:07:42.509 --> 00:07:46.053
to force them through
torture, to work for you,

00:07:46.053 --> 00:07:47.816
to buy and sell human beings,

00:07:47.816 --> 00:07:49.676
to sell children away from their parents.

00:07:49.676 --> 00:07:51.508
There was never a time where we didn't

00:07:51.508 --> 00:07:53.773
understand that was wrong.

00:07:53.773 --> 00:07:56.967
And if you read the writings
of many of the colonists

00:07:56.967 --> 00:07:58.961
at that time, for
instance, Thomas Jefferson,

00:07:58.961 --> 00:08:03.563
he's really dealing with that hypocrisy.

00:08:03.563 --> 00:08:08.563
I mean, he writes that
if there is a just God,

00:08:09.790 --> 00:08:14.017
America will pay for
the practice of slavery

00:08:14.017 --> 00:08:17.002
and you see that again and
again George Washington

00:08:17.002 --> 00:08:20.383
talks about being conflicted over

00:08:20.383 --> 00:08:22.270
this really barbaric practice.

00:08:22.270 --> 00:08:24.280
But the way that they
dealt with it is really

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:26.367
the way that we still
deal with it in society.

00:08:26.367 --> 00:08:31.367
Now we justify it, we kind
of fail to really grapple

00:08:32.422 --> 00:08:36.985
with what it means that nearly
half of the founding fathers

00:08:36.985 --> 00:08:40.933
owned human beings, that the
reason they had the wealth

00:08:40.933 --> 00:08:44.511
and the privilege and
the education to believe

00:08:44.511 --> 00:08:46.810
they could break off from the wealthiest,

00:08:46.810 --> 00:08:49.583
most powerful empire in
the world at the time,

00:08:49.583 --> 00:08:50.545
which was a British empire,

00:08:50.545 --> 00:08:53.224
was because of the money and profit

00:08:53.224 --> 00:08:55.865
they made off of human slavery.

00:08:55.865 --> 00:09:00.865
And we want to downplay that
because it is completely

00:09:02.852 --> 00:09:06.349
antithetical to the ideals of this country

00:09:06.349 --> 00:09:09.610
and that founding hypocrisy, you know,

00:09:09.610 --> 00:09:11.467
if you look at the Federalist Papers,

00:09:11.467 --> 00:09:14.506
and you look at how James Madison,

00:09:15.663 --> 00:09:17.621
considered the father of the constitution,

00:09:17.621 --> 00:09:20.428
they're trying to camouflage
what they're doing.

00:09:20.428 --> 00:09:24.345
They are trying to both sustain slavery,

00:09:24.345 --> 00:09:28.056
but in their documents
really erase slavery

00:09:28.056 --> 00:09:30.967
because they do understand that hypocrisy

00:09:30.967 --> 00:09:34.942
and actually Britain and
those who are loyalists

00:09:34.942 --> 00:09:38.513
seized upon that hypocrisy
when the colonists were making

00:09:38.513 --> 00:09:39.683
their case for freedom.

00:09:39.683 --> 00:09:41.410
And they were saying,
well, how can you be making

00:09:41.410 --> 00:09:44.500
a claim for freedom when you
trafficking in human beings?

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:47.509
And so a lot of people, when
they read the constitution,

00:09:47.509 --> 00:09:50.259
they'll say, well, the constitution
doesn't mention slavery.

00:09:50.259 --> 00:09:52.421
It does not, but that
was very intentional.

00:09:52.421 --> 00:09:55.343
We all know that the
3/5 compromise is about

00:09:55.343 --> 00:09:58.506
how do you count enslaved
people for representation?

00:09:58.506 --> 00:10:01.320
And yet you don't see the
word slavery in that document.

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:03.893
So they're doing this kind
of sleight of hand thing,

00:10:03.893 --> 00:10:08.893
because what makes America
unique is, it is not unique

00:10:09.383 --> 00:10:10.903
that we engaged in slavery.

00:10:10.903 --> 00:10:14.020
Yes, it is true that slavery
has an institution last,

00:10:14.020 --> 00:10:17.586
you know, predated America
by thousands of years,

00:10:17.586 --> 00:10:21.184
that many different cultures
and countries practice slavery.

00:10:21.184 --> 00:10:24.023
But we were the only
country founded on ideals

00:10:24.023 --> 00:10:26.938
of unalienable rights
of universal freedoms,

00:10:26.938 --> 00:10:29.835
that also was founded on slavery.

00:10:29.835 --> 00:10:32.793
We didn't just start practicing slavery

00:10:32.793 --> 00:10:34.143
at some point in our history.

00:10:34.143 --> 00:10:38.323
At our founding, we practiced
slavery while we were saying

00:10:38.323 --> 00:10:40.289
that we were a nation built on freedom,

00:10:40.289 --> 00:10:44.841
and 10 of the first 12 of our
presidents were enslavers.

00:10:44.841 --> 00:10:47.540
They forced people through torture

00:10:47.540 --> 00:10:51.243
to labor for them for free,
that's not incidental,

00:10:51.243 --> 00:10:54.272
even though we would like
to pretend that it is.

00:10:54.272 --> 00:10:56.570
- And you know, one of the
things and some of the imagery

00:10:56.570 --> 00:10:58.857
in your writing I think is super powerful.

00:10:58.857 --> 00:11:01.923
And I think the imagery really matters.

00:11:01.923 --> 00:11:03.514
There's a part in your introductory essay

00:11:03.514 --> 00:11:05.383
where you write about Thomas Jefferson,

00:11:05.383 --> 00:11:06.332
writing the Declaration of Independence

00:11:06.332 --> 00:11:10.364
which you would imagine as
this almost religious moment

00:11:10.364 --> 00:11:15.364
in our history, but he was
weighted on in Philadelphia

00:11:15.643 --> 00:11:20.643
by one of his slaves who
was his wife's half brother.

00:11:20.933 --> 00:11:25.111
- Yes, yes, so when we think about the way

00:11:25.111 --> 00:11:29.803
that we are taught history,
what we are not taught,

00:11:29.803 --> 00:11:31.649
what is left out is just as important

00:11:31.649 --> 00:11:34.788
as what is put in that story.

00:11:34.788 --> 00:11:38.326
And so when you're brought
up hearing the story

00:11:38.326 --> 00:11:41.316
of Thomas Jefferson writing
the Declaration of Independence

00:11:41.316 --> 00:11:46.316
in Philadelphia, the fact
is that yes, he owned 130

00:11:46.983 --> 00:11:49.943
human beings on his forced
labor camp in Monticello,

00:11:49.943 --> 00:11:53.235
which was built entirely
through forced labor

00:11:53.235 --> 00:11:55.169
at the time that he's writing

00:11:55.169 --> 00:11:57.153
these universal words of freedom,

00:11:57.153 --> 00:11:59.628
some of the most famous words
in the English language,

00:11:59.628 --> 00:12:01.116
most of us can say these words

00:12:01.116 --> 00:12:03.443
without even having to look at the paper,

00:12:03.443 --> 00:12:05.092
we hold these truths to be self evident,

00:12:05.092 --> 00:12:08.874
that all men are created
equal endowed by the creator

00:12:08.874 --> 00:12:10.703
with inalienable rights.

00:12:10.703 --> 00:12:13.845
These are majestic words,
but as he writes them,

00:12:13.845 --> 00:12:18.112
he knows that fully 1/5 of a
population in the 13 colonies

00:12:18.112 --> 00:12:20.697
will be deprived of all rights,

00:12:20.697 --> 00:12:22.634
including his own family members.

00:12:22.634 --> 00:12:26.926
So yes, he brings his wife's
half-brother who is a product

00:12:26.926 --> 00:12:31.863
of a sexual relationship
with his wife's father.

00:12:31.863 --> 00:12:33.851
And one of the enslaved
women that he owned,

00:12:33.851 --> 00:12:36.493
which of course by the nature of slavery

00:12:36.493 --> 00:12:38.578
was a coerced relationship,
and they own that.

00:12:38.578 --> 00:12:40.934
They own their own family members.

00:12:40.934 --> 00:12:44.583
And which was a very common
practice that enslavers would

00:12:44.583 --> 00:12:46.923
own their own children
as Thomas Jefferson did,

00:12:46.923 --> 00:12:48.536
they would own their own cousins.

00:12:48.536 --> 00:12:50.659
They would own their
own nieces and nephews.

00:12:50.659 --> 00:12:53.823
And he brought his wife's half brother

00:12:53.823 --> 00:12:58.557
to Philadelphia with him as
he's gonna draft this document

00:12:58.557 --> 00:13:01.819
for the world saying why the
colonists deserve to be free.

00:13:01.819 --> 00:13:05.517
And unfree man was taking
care of him and making sure

00:13:05.517 --> 00:13:06.767
he was comfortable.

00:13:06.767 --> 00:13:10.950
So we have to ask ourselves,
because the pushback of course,

00:13:10.950 --> 00:13:13.243
against The 1619 Project has been

00:13:13.243 --> 00:13:14.837
why are you rewriting history?

00:13:14.837 --> 00:13:17.017
Well, all of these are facts,

00:13:17.017 --> 00:13:19.733
but we've only learned
some of those facts.

00:13:19.733 --> 00:13:23.241
And it is an intentional
decision that we're gonna talk

00:13:23.241 --> 00:13:27.445
about Thomas Jefferson,
but not about the enslaved

00:13:27.445 --> 00:13:29.913
relative that he brought with him

00:13:29.913 --> 00:13:33.703
or the 130 enslaved people
who are back in Monticello

00:13:34.580 --> 00:13:37.103
creating the wealth that allowed him

00:13:37.103 --> 00:13:39.403
at the age of 33 or so years old,

00:13:39.403 --> 00:13:43.153
to be tasked with drafting of
the document that would allow

00:13:43.153 --> 00:13:45.953
us to leave the British empire.

00:13:45.953 --> 00:13:46.917
All of these facts matter,

00:13:46.917 --> 00:13:48.952
but we're only taught some of those facts.

00:13:48.952 --> 00:13:51.355
And the project is attempting to unearth

00:13:51.355 --> 00:13:53.754
a more complete story.

00:13:53.754 --> 00:13:55.829
- And how do you know,
as you just mentioned,

00:13:55.829 --> 00:13:56.938
it has been met with some critics,

00:13:56.938 --> 00:14:00.174
some of them very prominent historians.

00:14:00.174 --> 00:14:01.933
How do you reconcile?

00:14:01.933 --> 00:14:04.475
I have a reconciliation in
my head, I'm happy to share,

00:14:04.475 --> 00:14:06.177
but how do you reconcile that?

00:14:06.177 --> 00:14:08.433
It seems like we're in this
world where people think

00:14:08.433 --> 00:14:11.103
you either have to be ultra proud

00:14:11.103 --> 00:14:14.622
and think that your nation's
founding story is perfect

00:14:14.622 --> 00:14:18.603
and Holy in every way, or
if you don't believe that

00:14:18.603 --> 00:14:21.630
you're the cynic and you're
embarrassed of your country,

00:14:21.630 --> 00:14:24.279
isn't there a world where you can do both?

00:14:24.279 --> 00:14:26.093
You can be very proud of your country

00:14:26.093 --> 00:14:27.099
and where it's come from,

00:14:27.099 --> 00:14:29.403
but also know all of its wounds and know

00:14:29.403 --> 00:14:31.753
what all of the healing
that's left to be done.

00:14:32.943 --> 00:14:37.943
- Of course, it's been
interesting because people have,

00:14:37.983 --> 00:14:39.003
some of the critics have said

00:14:39.003 --> 00:14:41.512
that The 1619 Project is anti-American.

00:14:41.512 --> 00:14:45.993
I don't understand how anyone
can read my opening essay,

00:14:45.993 --> 00:14:47.360
which is on how black people,

00:14:47.360 --> 00:14:50.563
even though we were not
even considered humans

00:14:50.563 --> 00:14:53.450
or citizens of this country,
actually saw those words

00:14:53.450 --> 00:14:55.361
of the Declaration and the Constitution,

00:14:55.361 --> 00:14:58.569
and believed in them and
fought to make them true.

00:14:58.569 --> 00:15:02.078
And how we actually have
helped democratize America.

00:15:02.078 --> 00:15:05.080
That that is a patriotic essay that is not

00:15:05.080 --> 00:15:08.960
a project or an essay that
says we should hate America,

00:15:08.960 --> 00:15:12.627
but we certainly have to be
honest about where we came from.

00:15:12.627 --> 00:15:16.509
How do we explain why people
are in the streets right now?

00:15:16.509 --> 00:15:20.567
Having to proclaim to this
country that Black Lives Matter?

00:15:20.567 --> 00:15:23.657
When black people have
been here for 400 years,

00:15:23.657 --> 00:15:27.433
if we don't understand where we came from,

00:15:27.433 --> 00:15:30.625
and if we don't understand
that founding hypocrisy

00:15:30.625 --> 00:15:34.658
of what it means to be a country
founded in chattel slavery

00:15:34.658 --> 00:15:36.507
and ideas of freedom at the same time.

00:15:36.507 --> 00:15:39.151
We cannot be afraid of that.

00:15:39.151 --> 00:15:41.336
I understand, and we all know that the way

00:15:41.336 --> 00:15:42.773
that we're taught history

00:15:42.773 --> 00:15:45.090
is really a nationalistic endeavor

00:15:45.090 --> 00:15:48.390
It is as any country, the
history you teach yourself

00:15:48.390 --> 00:15:53.390
about your own country is
one to uplift the narrative

00:15:53.403 --> 00:15:54.756
of who you are as a people.

00:15:54.756 --> 00:15:59.305
And to really unite all of
us around a common sense

00:15:59.305 --> 00:16:01.435
and shared sense of identity,

00:16:01.435 --> 00:16:05.993
but that's simply impossible
without acknowledging

00:16:05.993 --> 00:16:08.457
the only reason black people
exist in the United States,

00:16:08.457 --> 00:16:09.582
is because of slavery.

00:16:09.582 --> 00:16:12.261
And so, because that is so inconvenient,

00:16:12.261 --> 00:16:14.712
we're largely just left out
of the story altogether,

00:16:14.712 --> 00:16:17.038
but then you can't
understand your country.

00:16:17.038 --> 00:16:20.958
You can't understand
who we are as a people,

00:16:20.958 --> 00:16:24.560
if you don't understand the entire truth.

00:16:24.560 --> 00:16:26.433
- Yeah, I mean, there's just so much even

00:16:26.433 --> 00:16:27.881
in your initial essay and much less

00:16:27.881 --> 00:16:29.243
than in the entire project,

00:16:29.243 --> 00:16:31.689
I mean, even this
notions that were Liberty

00:16:31.689 --> 00:16:35.393
from the British empire,
we kind of imagine

00:16:35.393 --> 00:16:36.921
this as this oppressive regime

00:16:36.921 --> 00:16:39.483
and they may were oppressive
in certain dimensions.

00:16:39.483 --> 00:16:41.173
Liberty without representation
and taxing things

00:16:41.173 --> 00:16:42.570
like tea and whatever else,

00:16:42.570 --> 00:16:45.311
but they abolished slavery
30 years in their colonies,

00:16:45.311 --> 00:16:46.709
30 years before we did.

00:16:46.709 --> 00:16:50.714
And they didn't do it by
force, they decided to do it.

00:16:50.714 --> 00:16:54.353
- That's right, I mean, even
at the time of the revolution,

00:16:54.353 --> 00:16:57.150
black people were picking
sides and black people

00:16:57.150 --> 00:16:59.159
were picking the side
where they felt they had

00:16:59.159 --> 00:17:01.073
the greatest chance of freedom.

00:17:01.073 --> 00:17:03.613
So lots of black people at
the time of the revolution

00:17:03.613 --> 00:17:06.676
actually fought on and
help beside of the British.

00:17:06.676 --> 00:17:09.528
They didn't have loyalty to the colonists.

00:17:09.528 --> 00:17:11.211
They didn't have loyalty to the British,

00:17:11.211 --> 00:17:13.118
they had loyalty to freedom.

00:17:13.118 --> 00:17:16.221
So you have to, again, really think about

00:17:16.221 --> 00:17:21.221
what does that mean to be told
a narrative of our founding?

00:17:21.624 --> 00:17:25.405
Which is that we were
founded because we believed

00:17:25.405 --> 00:17:26.791
in representative government,

00:17:26.791 --> 00:17:29.848
that people should be
able to rule themselves,

00:17:29.848 --> 00:17:33.860
that everyone should have a
right to choose their leaders.

00:17:33.860 --> 00:17:38.754
And yet we were denying all
of that to a large segment

00:17:38.754 --> 00:17:40.778
of the population here.

00:17:40.778 --> 00:17:43.805
There were lots of motivations
for the revolution.

00:17:43.805 --> 00:17:46.043
Some colonists has joined the revolution

00:17:46.043 --> 00:17:48.313
because the King had ordered
that they couldn't take

00:17:48.313 --> 00:17:51.500
any more indigenous land
and they wanted more land.

00:17:51.500 --> 00:17:54.658
Some joined it because they
did believe that people

00:17:54.658 --> 00:17:56.513
should have a right to self-governance.

00:17:56.513 --> 00:17:58.308
And some joined it
because they were worried

00:17:58.308 --> 00:18:01.011
that the British empire
was going to take away

00:18:01.011 --> 00:18:02.795
their right to enslave people.

00:18:02.795 --> 00:18:07.322
So we just have to get past
these very simplistic narratives

00:18:07.322 --> 00:18:10.763
that defy our founding,
that defy our founders.

00:18:10.763 --> 00:18:12.580
And one thing that I always ask people

00:18:12.580 --> 00:18:14.926
who say, well, you know, slavery was bad,

00:18:14.926 --> 00:18:17.736
but you have to look at the whole person.

00:18:19.853 --> 00:18:22.324
How would you think of someone who bought

00:18:22.324 --> 00:18:23.889
and sold you or your children?

00:18:23.889 --> 00:18:26.628
Would you look for the
good side of that person?

00:18:26.628 --> 00:18:28.658
Would you say, yeah but look at all

00:18:28.658 --> 00:18:31.422
the other great things that person did.

00:18:31.422 --> 00:18:33.549
We have to think about that in that way.

00:18:33.549 --> 00:18:37.614
I took my daughter to Thomas
Jefferson to Monticello,

00:18:37.614 --> 00:18:39.842
right before the COVID shutdowns.

00:18:39.842 --> 00:18:43.359
And we got a private tour
and one that really focused

00:18:43.359 --> 00:18:46.593
on the enslaved people
who built Monticello.

00:18:46.593 --> 00:18:49.213
And we go to the back of the
house and there's fingerprints

00:18:49.213 --> 00:18:53.804
in the bricks of enslaved
children who were forced

00:18:53.804 --> 00:18:55.433
instead of going to school like

00:18:55.433 --> 00:18:57.705
Thomas Jefferson's own children,

00:18:57.705 --> 00:19:00.461
these children were
having to through torture

00:19:00.461 --> 00:19:03.801
and the threat of violence
build and make the bricks

00:19:03.801 --> 00:19:05.805
that would build Thomas Jefferson's house.

00:19:05.805 --> 00:19:08.133
You can't ask us to ignore that.

00:19:08.133 --> 00:19:10.303
We just have to confront it and be honest

00:19:10.303 --> 00:19:12.217
about what that means.

00:19:12.217 --> 00:19:14.573
- No it's very powerful imagery.

00:19:14.573 --> 00:19:17.085
It's so easy to just have a
few sentences here and there.

00:19:17.085 --> 00:19:20.144
But when you really imagine those moments

00:19:20.144 --> 00:19:22.814
generation after generation
family separated,

00:19:22.814 --> 00:19:26.684
bought and sold, tortured
400 years of that,

00:19:26.684 --> 00:19:28.199
then it starts to sink in.

00:19:28.199 --> 00:19:31.153
And there's, there's so much,
I obviously I want people

00:19:31.153 --> 00:19:33.451
to read, look at the whole project,

00:19:33.451 --> 00:19:36.012
but just, even in my
research for this interview,

00:19:36.012 --> 00:19:38.653
some of the things that were
just mind blowing to me,

00:19:38.653 --> 00:19:40.750
Abraham Lincoln, I think for most of us,

00:19:40.750 --> 00:19:42.859
and I will say, still Thomas Jefferson

00:19:42.859 --> 00:19:45.016
and Abraham Lincoln are kind of these,

00:19:46.053 --> 00:19:48.555
larger than life figures at America.

00:19:48.555 --> 00:19:51.674
And Abraham Lincoln, seems to have been

00:19:51.674 --> 00:19:55.471
a very forward thinking
person for his time.

00:19:55.471 --> 00:20:00.471
But even Abraham Lincoln
was not without issues.

00:20:01.053 --> 00:20:05.841
He asked a group of African-American
leaders that if they

00:20:05.841 --> 00:20:09.337
were liberated that
perhaps they should leave.

00:20:09.337 --> 00:20:14.337
- Yes, Abraham Lincoln until
a few years before his death

00:20:14.973 --> 00:20:16.179
leaving colonization.

00:20:16.179 --> 00:20:19.320
He believed that black
people were incompatible

00:20:19.320 --> 00:20:23.709
with white democracy and
that there were too many

00:20:23.709 --> 00:20:26.943
hard feelings and black and
my people were too different.

00:20:26.943 --> 00:20:30.133
So the only way that black
people could be free would

00:20:30.133 --> 00:20:32.608
be if they were to leave the
United States altogether.

00:20:32.608 --> 00:20:35.823
Now keep in mind, of
course that black people

00:20:35.823 --> 00:20:38.667
have been in this country
for hundreds of years.

00:20:38.667 --> 00:20:42.236
And also through the process of slavery

00:20:42.236 --> 00:20:45.274
had lost any connection
to any other country.

00:20:45.274 --> 00:20:49.049
So there was nowhere for
black Americans to go back to,

00:20:49.049 --> 00:20:50.956
even in the way of European immigrants

00:20:50.956 --> 00:20:54.219
or European immigrants all knew
what country they came from,

00:20:54.219 --> 00:20:56.253
what language they used to speak,

00:20:56.253 --> 00:20:57.755
but black people lost all of that.

00:20:57.755 --> 00:21:02.193
So America really was our only
country and the man who we've

00:21:02.193 --> 00:21:04.821
all been taught to think
of as a great emancipator.

00:21:04.821 --> 00:21:09.043
And he was a great emancipator,
but he was a human being.

00:21:09.043 --> 00:21:13.053
He was a man who like most
white Americans at that time,

00:21:13.053 --> 00:21:14.883
believed that black people were inferior

00:21:14.883 --> 00:21:18.853
and he actually blamed black
people for the civil war.

00:21:18.853 --> 00:21:22.728
He actually says to those
men, if you were not here,

00:21:22.728 --> 00:21:27.010
we wouldn't be fighting our
white brothers in South,

00:21:27.010 --> 00:21:29.043
as a black people wanted to be

00:21:29.043 --> 00:21:30.878
in the United States and enslaved.

00:21:30.878 --> 00:21:35.489
So again, that your
reaction is the reaction

00:21:35.489 --> 00:21:38.843
we've gotten again and
again to The 1619 Project

00:21:38.843 --> 00:21:40.825
is I just didn't know, I had no idea.

00:21:40.825 --> 00:21:44.367
And I think that that
troubling of the narrative

00:21:44.367 --> 00:21:47.379
is so critical because how do we address

00:21:47.379 --> 00:21:50.003
the ongoing inequality and
the legacy of that If we don't

00:21:50.003 --> 00:21:52.993
acknowledge what actually happened?

00:21:52.993 --> 00:21:55.536
If we don't know what actually happened.

00:21:55.536 --> 00:21:59.903
- And I think if I say there's
one theme of The 1619 Project

00:21:59.903 --> 00:22:01.340
which was really eyeopening for me

00:22:01.340 --> 00:22:05.910
is when we learn American
history, we look at, okay some

00:22:05.910 --> 00:22:08.312
great words were written on
the Declaration of Independence

00:22:08.312 --> 00:22:09.300
and in the constitution.

00:22:09.300 --> 00:22:10.976
And in other kinds of
supporting documents,

00:22:10.976 --> 00:22:13.901
Federalist Papers, other things
like it, but we say, okay,

00:22:13.901 --> 00:22:16.439
but people were of their
time, it was kinda a flawed

00:22:16.439 --> 00:22:18.912
history but look those
words helped us evolve

00:22:18.912 --> 00:22:21.212
to become a more perfect
union and look at us

00:22:21.212 --> 00:22:23.417
decade by decade, although to your point

00:22:23.417 --> 00:22:26.093
there was definitely some
regressive moments, but it seems

00:22:26.093 --> 00:22:27.020
like we're getting better.
- Well, we're not

00:22:27.020 --> 00:22:32.020
- But to your point, we haven't
evolved just because those

00:22:33.910 --> 00:22:35.662
words were written in the
Declaration of Independence

00:22:35.662 --> 00:22:36.904
or the constitution.

00:22:36.904 --> 00:22:41.904
It was a struggle for
essentially Black Americans

00:22:42.462 --> 00:22:47.462
over the last 240 years
since our founding to make

00:22:48.477 --> 00:22:52.399
those words less
hypocritical or to make them,

00:22:52.399 --> 00:22:55.573
all the ironies so that we
get closer to the reality.

00:22:55.573 --> 00:22:59.270
I mean, I love this quote from your essay,

00:22:59.270 --> 00:23:02.249
"What if America understood
finally, in this 400th year,

00:23:02.249 --> 00:23:05.304
so 400 years after 1619,
that we have never been

00:23:05.304 --> 00:23:08.611
the problem, but the solution?"

00:23:08.611 --> 00:23:10.483
I thought that was a super powerful.

00:23:10.483 --> 00:23:14.359
And I'm speaking as an immigrant
who kind of got benefits

00:23:14.359 --> 00:23:18.596
from the fruits of us
becoming a more perfect union.

00:23:18.596 --> 00:23:23.188
When my family immigrated in
the late '60s and early '70s

00:23:23.188 --> 00:23:25.222
explain what you mean by
never been the problem,

00:23:25.222 --> 00:23:27.003
but the solution

00:23:27.003 --> 00:23:32.003
- Right, so if you do a Google
search on the Negro problem,

00:23:32.313 --> 00:23:34.705
the black problem, you will
find an unending number

00:23:34.705 --> 00:23:39.053
of articles, whether they
be journalistic articles,

00:23:39.053 --> 00:23:42.783
social science articles,
trying to resolve the problem

00:23:42.783 --> 00:23:44.723
with black people, what is
wrong with black people?

00:23:44.723 --> 00:23:48.131
And I really assert in this
project that there's nothing

00:23:48.131 --> 00:23:49.483
wrong with black people.

00:23:49.483 --> 00:23:52.148
There's certainly wrong with
a country that would enslave

00:23:52.148 --> 00:23:57.148
human beings for profit and
all of the statistics and data

00:23:57.410 --> 00:23:59.562
that we use to say that there's
a problem with black people

00:23:59.562 --> 00:24:01.723
are perfectly predictable in a country

00:24:01.723 --> 00:24:03.429
built on chattel slavery.

00:24:03.429 --> 00:24:06.299
But when we think of the
role of black people,

00:24:06.299 --> 00:24:09.283
we never think about that
black people have actually been

00:24:09.283 --> 00:24:12.423
the primary democratizing
force in this country

00:24:12.423 --> 00:24:15.169
that ever since they read those words

00:24:15.169 --> 00:24:18.399
of the declaration, they
took those words literally.

00:24:18.399 --> 00:24:20.382
They didn't see them as some great ideas

00:24:20.382 --> 00:24:22.000
that looked good on paper.

00:24:22.000 --> 00:24:25.891
They actually believed that
all men and women are created

00:24:25.891 --> 00:24:27.825
equal and that every human being

00:24:27.825 --> 00:24:30.213
is entitled to universal rights.

00:24:30.213 --> 00:24:33.226
And you see them using this
language of universal rights

00:24:33.226 --> 00:24:35.859
before anyone else is really using it,

00:24:35.859 --> 00:24:37.943
even before the declaration.

00:24:37.943 --> 00:24:41.434
So we think about this history,
it has been Black Americans

00:24:41.434 --> 00:24:43.765
through bloody struggle
after bloody struggle

00:24:43.765 --> 00:24:47.335
in the United States
against their own countrymen

00:24:47.335 --> 00:24:50.073
have fought really to
make those ideas true.

00:24:50.073 --> 00:24:54.853
The period after the civil
war known as reconstruction,

00:24:54.853 --> 00:24:58.343
which is this brief
12-year period where we see

00:24:58.343 --> 00:24:59.675
this large expansion of rights.

00:24:59.675 --> 00:25:02.373
We get the end abolishment of slavery

00:25:02.373 --> 00:25:04.515
with the 13th amendment,
the 14th amendment,

00:25:04.515 --> 00:25:07.926
which is the equal protection
of the constitution.

00:25:07.926 --> 00:25:10.361
And then the 15th amendment,
which says you can't deny

00:25:10.361 --> 00:25:12.554
people the right to vote
because of their race.

00:25:12.554 --> 00:25:16.673
This all comes because of the organizing

00:25:16.673 --> 00:25:20.921
of formerly enslaved people
to ensure equal rights

00:25:20.921 --> 00:25:23.003
for anyone regardless of race.

00:25:23.003 --> 00:25:26.792
And then you see that what
we know as the civil rights

00:25:26.792 --> 00:25:30.203
struggle, which once again through the law

00:25:30.203 --> 00:25:33.033
is trying to end legal
discrimination and force the country

00:25:33.033 --> 00:25:34.687
to live up to its founding ideals.

00:25:34.687 --> 00:25:37.865
Today, we're burying one
of the greatest Americans

00:25:37.865 --> 00:25:39.576
that this country has produced.

00:25:39.576 --> 00:25:42.504
And without someone like John Lewis

00:25:42.504 --> 00:25:44.655
and Dr. King and Diane Nash.

00:25:44.655 --> 00:25:47.543
We couldn't call ourselves a democracy

00:25:47.543 --> 00:25:50.274
because this was a country
that was going abroad

00:25:50.274 --> 00:25:53.172
and fighting fascism, fighting Nazis,

00:25:53.172 --> 00:25:56.745
fighting other countries to
democratize them while brutally

00:25:56.745 --> 00:25:59.706
denying democracy to its own citizens.

00:25:59.706 --> 00:26:02.923
Even your own story,
your family being able

00:26:02.923 --> 00:26:04.810
to immigrate here in the late 1960s,

00:26:04.810 --> 00:26:08.583
early '70s is because of the
black resistance struggle.

00:26:08.583 --> 00:26:10.728
Prior to 1965, this country

00:26:10.728 --> 00:26:13.581
had a racist immigration quota system.

00:26:13.581 --> 00:26:17.104
And it denied nearly
all immigrants of color

00:26:17.104 --> 00:26:18.833
from entering the United States

00:26:18.833 --> 00:26:22.183
in order to keep the
United States more white

00:26:22.183 --> 00:26:24.013
because of the civil rights movement,

00:26:24.013 --> 00:26:27.101
we passed the 1965 immigration
and nationalization act,

00:26:27.101 --> 00:26:29.631
and it ends that racist
immigration quota system.

00:26:29.631 --> 00:26:33.646
And that's when you start to
see large numbers of Asian,

00:26:33.646 --> 00:26:37.413
African and South American
and Caribbean immigrants being

00:26:37.413 --> 00:26:39.833
allowed into the U S for the first time.

00:26:39.833 --> 00:26:42.971
And on top of that, when
your family finally got here,

00:26:42.971 --> 00:26:45.413
because of the black resistance struggle,

00:26:45.413 --> 00:26:49.148
all legal discrimination
was ended through the law.

00:26:49.148 --> 00:26:51.917
So you were not only able to come,

00:26:51.917 --> 00:26:54.281
but to enter into a country
where it was no longer legal

00:26:54.281 --> 00:26:55.803
to discriminate against you

00:26:55.803 --> 00:26:57.903
because of your race or national origin.

00:26:57.903 --> 00:27:01.028
And this was all because of
the black resistance struggle,

00:27:01.028 --> 00:27:03.436
but we're not taught
that history that way.

00:27:03.436 --> 00:27:06.043
And we're not taught that
history in a way that not only

00:27:06.043 --> 00:27:09.204
were black people fighting
for their own rights,

00:27:09.204 --> 00:27:11.339
but they were fighting to democratize

00:27:11.339 --> 00:27:14.012
and make equal our entire country.

00:27:14.012 --> 00:27:16.176
Just the last point I'll add on that.

00:27:17.263 --> 00:27:18.823
I make this point in my essay,

00:27:18.823 --> 00:27:22.341
every other marginalized
group, whether it be a women,

00:27:22.341 --> 00:27:24.792
whether it be those who are disabled,

00:27:24.792 --> 00:27:29.338
whether it be gay Americans
or immigrants have all used

00:27:29.338 --> 00:27:32.150
the tools and the tactics
and the rights expansions

00:27:32.150 --> 00:27:33.945
of the black civil rights movements

00:27:33.945 --> 00:27:36.283
to expand their own rights.

00:27:36.283 --> 00:27:39.623
And probably the best
examples of that is when

00:27:39.623 --> 00:27:41.110
the Supreme Court ruled

00:27:41.110 --> 00:27:44.899
that the gay marriage was constitutional.

00:27:44.899 --> 00:27:48.973
They use the 14th amendment
equal protection clause

00:27:48.973 --> 00:27:52.188
pushed by formerly enslaved
in order to make that ruling.

00:27:52.188 --> 00:27:55.018
And when recently the Supreme Court ruled

00:27:55.018 --> 00:27:57.173
that it was illegal to discriminate

00:27:57.173 --> 00:27:58.960
against gay Americans and employment,

00:27:58.960 --> 00:28:03.393
they use the 1964 Civil
rights Acts, pushed again

00:28:03.393 --> 00:28:05.923
through the resistance
and civil rights struggles

00:28:05.923 --> 00:28:07.883
of black Americans to make that ruling.

00:28:07.883 --> 00:28:09.896
So when I made that sentence,

00:28:09.896 --> 00:28:12.844
I was simply arguing we are all taught.

00:28:12.844 --> 00:28:15.104
And I actually think for
immigrants that is part

00:28:15.104 --> 00:28:17.233
of the Americanization process

00:28:17.233 --> 00:28:18.984
is to be taught
anti-blackness to be taught

00:28:18.984 --> 00:28:20.685
that black people are a problem.

00:28:20.685 --> 00:28:24.093
And you have to separate
yourself from these problematic

00:28:24.093 --> 00:28:26.730
people if you want to succeed in America,

00:28:26.730 --> 00:28:31.093
when in fact we have
then the equalizing force

00:28:31.093 --> 00:28:33.483
in this country and what
would it mean if we thought

00:28:33.483 --> 00:28:35.126
about black people in that way?

00:28:36.176 --> 00:28:39.043
- I think that's so powerful,
I will say very openly coming

00:28:39.043 --> 00:28:40.793
from an immigrant Asian community,

00:28:40.793 --> 00:28:43.996
the community does not realize this.

00:28:44.863 --> 00:28:47.633
I've gotten into arguments
at Thanksgiving dinners

00:28:47.633 --> 00:28:48.604
saying you don't realize.

00:28:48.604 --> 00:28:50.340
And I think there's sometimes a narrative

00:28:50.340 --> 00:28:52.923
and immigrant community is
like, look, we came here,

00:28:52.923 --> 00:28:56.274
we just had $8 in our pocket
and look at what we did.

00:28:56.274 --> 00:29:01.133
Well, first of all you were
kinda creamed off the crop,

00:29:01.133 --> 00:29:02.937
cream of the crop but in
that country where you got

00:29:02.937 --> 00:29:05.413
the education, et cetera, et cetera.

00:29:05.413 --> 00:29:09.163
You came, you did not
have 400 years of families

00:29:09.163 --> 00:29:10.468
being torn apart, being torn away

00:29:10.468 --> 00:29:12.900
from your cultural legacy,
et cetera et cetera.

00:29:12.900 --> 00:29:14.991
And then you were able
to come to this country

00:29:14.991 --> 00:29:17.577
really on the foundation
that you just laid.

00:29:17.577 --> 00:29:18.643
You're absolutely true.

00:29:18.643 --> 00:29:21.527
I mean my father immigrated
this country in 1968.

00:29:21.527 --> 00:29:24.383
Exactly for the reasons you
came, he came, he was a medical

00:29:24.383 --> 00:29:27.293
professional and we had
a shortage at the time,

00:29:27.293 --> 00:29:30.743
but if he would not have
been allowed to come

00:29:30.743 --> 00:29:31.995
five or six years before that,

00:29:31.995 --> 00:29:36.093
and all of what applies around
segregation and everything

00:29:36.093 --> 00:29:37.592
would have likely applied to my father

00:29:37.592 --> 00:29:40.150
we don't know.
- Absolutely.

00:29:40.150 --> 00:29:42.913
We weren't allowed.

00:29:42.913 --> 00:29:45.283
So I think a lot of the
immigrant communities

00:29:45.283 --> 00:29:49.633
need to internalize how
much of what we have

00:29:49.633 --> 00:29:51.738
has been built on this foundation.

00:29:51.738 --> 00:29:54.454
I do wanna make sure that
we get so many incredible

00:29:54.454 --> 00:29:55.287
questions coming out.

00:29:55.287 --> 00:30:00.283
So from Facebook, Carol Smith Kapur says,

00:30:00.283 --> 00:30:02.323
what is your reaction to
Tom Cotton's assertion

00:30:02.323 --> 00:30:06.076
that The 1619 Project is going
to lead to racial division.

00:30:09.173 --> 00:30:11.311
- We have a white nationalist president

00:30:11.311 --> 00:30:15.190
in the White House right now
and for more than two months,

00:30:15.190 --> 00:30:18.803
we've had people marching in
the streets trying to make

00:30:18.803 --> 00:30:20.653
the statement that Black Lives Matter.

00:30:20.653 --> 00:30:25.015
So we are in racially
divided country already.

00:30:25.015 --> 00:30:28.543
We know that Donald Trump won

00:30:28.543 --> 00:30:30.375
with virtually all white supports.

00:30:30.375 --> 00:30:35.375
He lost the majority of
black, Asian, Latino,

00:30:35.613 --> 00:30:38.332
and native voters and
he has run on a pretty

00:30:38.332 --> 00:30:40.457
openly racist campaign.

00:30:40.457 --> 00:30:43.629
So when Tom Cotton says
something like that,

00:30:43.629 --> 00:30:45.906
it's not anything that I take seriously.

00:30:45.906 --> 00:30:49.937
What he's really saying
is if we actually tell

00:30:49.937 --> 00:30:51.414
the truth about our country,

00:30:51.414 --> 00:30:54.091
that is something that we
can't handle as Americans.

00:30:54.091 --> 00:30:56.493
And that's divisive,

00:30:56.493 --> 00:30:59.168
but I don't think confronting the facts

00:30:59.168 --> 00:31:02.146
about who we are is ever
going to be divisive.

00:31:02.146 --> 00:31:06.603
And in fact, you cannot heal
a racial rift if you ignore

00:31:06.603 --> 00:31:07.756
what has caused it.

00:31:08.890 --> 00:31:10.669
- Yeah and I'll just underline that.

00:31:10.669 --> 00:31:13.454
I mean, I'm confused by comments

00:31:13.454 --> 00:31:15.220
like that because
especially The 1619 Project,

00:31:15.220 --> 00:31:17.754
the way that it was done,
it is really just knowledge

00:31:17.754 --> 00:31:21.853
and information about our shared history.

00:31:21.853 --> 00:31:25.689
And it's kind of like,
I don't want to make,

00:31:25.689 --> 00:31:29.289
when you have a good friend,
but there's something

00:31:29.289 --> 00:31:31.573
that's kind of inside of you.

00:31:31.573 --> 00:31:34.453
And one of them that they
have a problem with it doesn't

00:31:34.453 --> 00:31:38.133
help anyone to just
sweep it under the rug.

00:31:38.133 --> 00:31:41.155
It's always healthy, any
psychotherapist will tell you,

00:31:41.155 --> 00:31:44.592
it's healthy to say look, this
happened, this is a reality.

00:31:44.592 --> 00:31:46.323
We need to talk about it.

00:31:46.323 --> 00:31:48.643
And I think that's all this
project is, it's not saying

00:31:48.643 --> 00:31:50.239
don't be incredibly proud of your country

00:31:50.239 --> 00:31:53.743
don't be patriotic, it's saying the most

00:31:53.743 --> 00:31:57.443
patriotic thing we can do
is to really understand

00:31:57.443 --> 00:31:58.538
our good, the bad, the ugly,

00:31:58.538 --> 00:32:00.098
the same thing about your family.

00:32:00.098 --> 00:32:02.035
All of our families,
we have great stories.

00:32:02.035 --> 00:32:05.533
We have not so great stories,
we have to own all of them.

00:32:05.533 --> 00:32:06.541
And then we actually become a family,

00:32:06.541 --> 00:32:08.983
I think is really what's going on here.

00:32:08.983 --> 00:32:12.231
Maybe let's see one more question

00:32:12.231 --> 00:32:13.707
or one or two if you have time.

00:32:13.707 --> 00:32:15.830
From Facebook, Adrian T. White says,

00:32:15.830 --> 00:32:17.021
"As a black man in the South,

00:32:17.021 --> 00:32:19.783
I remember our elementary
school teacher only gave us

00:32:19.783 --> 00:32:21.925
about one chapter about slavery
and indentured servitude.

00:32:21.925 --> 00:32:23.852
And we didn't get to learn about our past.

00:32:23.852 --> 00:32:25.093
I even remember the publisher

00:32:25.093 --> 00:32:27.437
of our social studies
book, Houghton Mifflin.

00:32:27.437 --> 00:32:30.701
How can we help neutralize
some of the systemic plans

00:32:30.701 --> 00:32:32.923
to leave us out of the conversations?

00:32:32.923 --> 00:32:36.875
It's so empowering to
know where you came from

00:32:36.875 --> 00:32:39.868
and I'll just add, do you think
this is intentionally done?

00:32:39.868 --> 00:32:43.232
Or do you think it's just kind
of built kind of a momentum

00:32:43.232 --> 00:32:45.243
of itself and it's it's unintentional

00:32:45.243 --> 00:32:47.360
or it's kind of a combination?

00:32:47.360 --> 00:32:49.556
- I definitely think it's intentional.

00:32:51.248 --> 00:32:55.543
The history is available, so
nothing in The 1619 Project

00:32:55.543 --> 00:32:58.203
that I just singlehandedly unearth.

00:32:58.203 --> 00:33:00.895
We got everything from
studying the history books

00:33:00.895 --> 00:33:02.993
that have been written about this.

00:33:02.993 --> 00:33:07.379
But when you think about
again, what is the role

00:33:07.379 --> 00:33:11.503
of US history as taught in K-12 education?

00:33:11.503 --> 00:33:14.210
And it is to create a
shared national narrative.

00:33:14.210 --> 00:33:18.113
So you necessarily are
playing up the parts

00:33:18.113 --> 00:33:20.240
that glorify your country
and you're playing down

00:33:20.240 --> 00:33:22.123
the parts that do not.

00:33:22.123 --> 00:33:24.238
So you don't get very
much about what happened

00:33:24.238 --> 00:33:27.963
to native people, you don't
get very much about slavery.

00:33:27.963 --> 00:33:29.943
There's a great deal of discomfort,

00:33:29.943 --> 00:33:32.107
with actually dealing
with what that means.

00:33:32.107 --> 00:33:34.645
And I do think that's intentional.

00:33:34.645 --> 00:33:38.754
I talk about a lot how I took my first

00:33:38.754 --> 00:33:41.023
African-American studies course.

00:33:41.023 --> 00:33:42.567
It was an elective course in high school

00:33:42.567 --> 00:33:46.423
and how transformative
that class was for me,

00:33:46.423 --> 00:33:49.113
because in that one
semester, I learned more

00:33:49.113 --> 00:33:51.333
about the history and
contributions of people,

00:33:51.333 --> 00:33:52.913
of African descent than I had learned

00:33:52.913 --> 00:33:55.963
in my entire education
up until that point.

00:33:55.963 --> 00:33:59.350
And it's a very demeaning
thing to be taught

00:33:59.350 --> 00:34:02.123
that the only contributions
black people ever made

00:34:02.123 --> 00:34:03.153
is someone owned them.

00:34:03.153 --> 00:34:05.114
And then this great white man freed them.

00:34:05.114 --> 00:34:08.093
And then that's the end of your story.

00:34:08.093 --> 00:34:11.120
And so to have a different view of that,

00:34:11.120 --> 00:34:13.584
and a more full history
and I wish there could be

00:34:13.584 --> 00:34:17.173
a 1619 project for indigenous people

00:34:17.173 --> 00:34:18.923
and their stories as well.

00:34:18.923 --> 00:34:22.723
And for Asian people, there's
just, the beauty of America

00:34:22.723 --> 00:34:26.108
is it is this great multiracial nation.

00:34:26.108 --> 00:34:28.503
And it was founded on these amazing ideas.

00:34:28.503 --> 00:34:30.542
We just fail to live up to them.

00:34:30.542 --> 00:34:34.353
And the most patriotic thing
you can do is to fight,

00:34:34.353 --> 00:34:36.720
to make those ideals real and true,

00:34:36.720 --> 00:34:39.226
not to ignore all of the failings.

00:34:39.226 --> 00:34:44.226
So I think one thing that we
have done with The 1619 Project

00:34:45.523 --> 00:34:49.516
is we partner with the Pulitzer
Center on Crisis Reporting,

00:34:49.516 --> 00:34:52.563
and they've created an
entire free curriculum.

00:34:52.563 --> 00:34:54.720
And that curriculum is
available at the Pulitzer Center

00:34:54.720 --> 00:34:57.183
and teachers all across
the country are using

00:34:57.183 --> 00:35:00.376
that to supplement the standard
curriculum that they have

00:35:00.376 --> 00:35:03.699
to give students a broader perspective,

00:35:03.699 --> 00:35:07.228
but also just to teach them the question.

00:35:07.228 --> 00:35:09.684
So every time you're
exposed to a narrative

00:35:09.684 --> 00:35:10.996
that you hadn't heard before,

00:35:10.996 --> 00:35:13.169
you don't have to believe that narrative,

00:35:13.169 --> 00:35:15.593
but it should cause you then to question,

00:35:15.593 --> 00:35:18.353
what are we taught and why and
what am I not being taught?

00:35:18.353 --> 00:35:20.864
And hopefully to seek out more
knowledge and Khan Academy

00:35:20.864 --> 00:35:24.661
is such a great stage for us
to have this conversation,

00:35:24.661 --> 00:35:26.539
because that is exactly
what you're built on,

00:35:26.539 --> 00:35:29.418
is helping people to seek out knowledge

00:35:29.418 --> 00:35:31.383
and different ways of
understanding things.

00:35:31.383 --> 00:35:34.600
And that's what we're trying
to do with the project as well.

00:35:34.600 --> 00:35:36.663
- That's so powerful and
maybe one last question.

00:35:36.663 --> 00:35:38.093
This is I think an interesting,

00:35:38.093 --> 00:35:39.308
forward-looking question.

00:35:39.308 --> 00:35:41.064
For actually there's two
questions here related

00:35:41.064 --> 00:35:44.383
from YouTube, AZ asks,
why do you think people

00:35:44.383 --> 00:35:46.519
still have a racist mindset?

00:35:46.519 --> 00:35:49.933
And do you think racial
equality will ever be possible?

00:35:49.933 --> 00:35:52.373
I was also asked, do you
think Martin Luther King's

00:35:52.373 --> 00:35:53.234
dream will come true?

00:35:53.234 --> 00:35:56.578
And we will be, you know, race
won't matter at some point

00:35:56.578 --> 00:35:58.586
or will be racially equal?

00:36:03.673 --> 00:36:06.084
- So those are two kinds
of separate questions.

00:36:06.084 --> 00:36:09.976
Do I think that racial
equality is possible?

00:36:09.976 --> 00:36:13.692
Yes, of course it's possible
because the racial inequality

00:36:13.692 --> 00:36:17.495
that we see is not natural,
it's not inevitable,

00:36:17.495 --> 00:36:19.382
it was created and it's sustained.

00:36:19.382 --> 00:36:22.803
So if we chose to make a different choice,

00:36:22.803 --> 00:36:26.333
if we decided to put in
the type of resources

00:36:26.333 --> 00:36:29.801
to undoing inequality as
we did to creating it,

00:36:29.801 --> 00:36:32.380
then we certainly could get rid of it.

00:36:32.380 --> 00:36:35.153
But do I think that will do that?

00:36:35.153 --> 00:36:37.753
Well, you know, The 1619
Projects is celebrating

00:36:37.753 --> 00:36:39.906
the 400th year of this struggle,

00:36:39.906 --> 00:36:44.906
and now the 401st year and
black people are still trying

00:36:46.823 --> 00:36:49.369
to fight to say our lives
matter, that we shouldn't be

00:36:49.369 --> 00:36:51.513
killed by the police,
black people are dying

00:36:51.513 --> 00:36:53.793
at the highest rates from
COVID, black people have

00:36:53.793 --> 00:36:55.799
the highest rates of
unemployment right now.

00:36:55.799 --> 00:36:59.744
The highest rates of people who
are unable to pay their rent

00:36:59.744 --> 00:37:01.463
and mortgage, because of COVID.

00:37:01.463 --> 00:37:06.463
So I don't think that if we
are not willing to confront

00:37:07.844 --> 00:37:11.742
our past and then to do
something very expensive,

00:37:11.742 --> 00:37:15.476
to address the disadvantage,
that created this disadvantage

00:37:15.476 --> 00:37:18.452
that we will ever achieve equal society.

00:37:18.452 --> 00:37:21.734
But we certainly are all charged
with trying to help us get

00:37:21.734 --> 00:37:25.106
closer to the society that I won't say

00:37:25.106 --> 00:37:27.213
that our founders envisioned,

00:37:27.213 --> 00:37:31.136
but that our founders laid down on paper.

00:37:32.260 --> 00:37:35.109
- I think that's a very
very very powerful ideas.

00:37:35.109 --> 00:37:37.809
And it'll be clear something
that we have to work for.

00:37:38.843 --> 00:37:40.953
Nikole, thank you so much for joining.

00:37:40.953 --> 00:37:42.740
I think, just in the research
for this conversation,

00:37:42.740 --> 00:37:44.540
I learned so much and
obviously talking to you,

00:37:44.540 --> 00:37:47.078
that's so much depth and
texture really appreciate it.

00:37:47.078 --> 00:37:48.913
Thank you for all the
work you've been doing.

00:37:48.913 --> 00:37:50.490
I mean before The 1619 Project,

00:37:50.490 --> 00:37:53.223
but especially The 1619 Project,

00:37:53.223 --> 00:37:55.609
I think is an important part
of our collective education.

00:37:55.609 --> 00:37:59.963
- Thank you so much for hosting me.

00:37:59.963 --> 00:38:02.881
- So everyone, thanks again
for joining the live stream.

00:38:02.881 --> 00:38:07.204
As you could see, a really
powerful conversation

00:38:07.204 --> 00:38:10.913
with Nikole and The 1619
Project, I learned a lot.

00:38:10.913 --> 00:38:12.983
I thought I knew a lot
about American history

00:38:12.983 --> 00:38:17.525
and my big takeaway here is
if I continue to be confused,

00:38:17.525 --> 00:38:21.449
why anyone would believe
that learning more about our

00:38:21.449 --> 00:38:26.449
history with somehow polarize
or somehow demean our history.

00:38:27.895 --> 00:38:30.123
It gives us more and more ownership

00:38:30.123 --> 00:38:31.893
from where we come from collectively.

00:38:31.893 --> 00:38:35.967
And so I think projects like
this are not only important.

00:38:35.967 --> 00:38:38.998
I think they're essential, so
thanks so much for joining.

00:38:38.998 --> 00:38:41.565
And I look forward to seeing
y'all I think next week

00:38:41.565 --> 00:38:43.726
on this live stream, see you then.

00:38:47.557 --> 00:38:50.140
(logo chiming)

